<p>As others have said, this isn't bad for a first SCP. There are some possible areas for improvement, but It's a cool idea and it all hangs together pretty well.</p>
<p>+1</p>
<p>A few comments:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The author, R. Sebastian, has authored several other anomalies; attempts to track and capture this person are ongoing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Minor note, but a some other articles would provide a person of interest number, like &quot;(designated PoI-####)&quot;, for the author. It's not really necessary, more of a personal/style choice, just mentioning.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Sustained interaction with SCP-3039-1 has proven deleterious to mental health, causing intense anger and poor decision making in subjects. This is theorized to be an additional effect of SCP-3039-1, and not human reaction to the anomaly, due to the extreme shifts in the typical composure of several test subjects.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You've disarmed me a bit here. Usually I tell authors that having people massively and angrily overreact to something is an unrealistic portrayal of human behavior, and that SCPs that mentally influence everyone to act in a specific way are poor writing. Those things are both present, but I like the balance here. I do feel for Teals, which is what both of those criticisms are meant to improve.</p>
<p>Intense anger and poor decision-making aren't exactly technical terms from mental health, though.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Experiment 15: Procedure: Subject exposed to a cognitohazard attempting to counter SCP-3039.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a pretty vague. Like, dI'd they create one to just nullify it, or were they trying to alter behavior? It's just a little odd to rely so much on them being able to build cognitohazards without just a smidge more detail.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Procedure: Subject opens SCP-3039’s storage locker and inflicts significant damage to several instances with an axe while screaming counter cognitohazard, “[EXPLETIVE] you, you [EXPLETIVE], blocky [EXPLETIVE]. Let me [EXPLETIVE] write, you [EXPLETIVE]!”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So&#8230; The Foundation has a cognitohazard that is just angry swearing? Also, you don't have to remove expletives. If you were redacting them because knowing the exact expletives and order constitutes a cognitohazard, that's funny enough to warrant a footnote.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Reprimand aside, this incident shows the anomaly has no degree of sentience as previously theorized, and that the effect is only provoked under certain circumstances.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't understand how it shows this, or how yelling a cognitohazard at it was going to cause anything, even if it was sentient. It was funny, though.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Not really, as it not even a physical object, but I like to imagine it does.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Typo here.</p>
</div>
