<li>I think I'll do that.</li>
<li>Glad that bit didn't come off as too contrived. Will make changes for more clinical tone to address the second part.</li>
<li>I'll add some more detail to that.</li>
<li>No, the profanity isn't actually a cognitohazard, but if you were an angry researcher trying to cover his words and actions (which in this case, were truly extreme and inappropriate), wouldn't you make something to that effect up? While highly unethical, I would. I'll think about removing the censoring, but I'd like to think that it's the Foundation equivalent of &quot;I don't believe you, but I also don't care about calling you out.&quot; Your final comment there makes me want to add that. I think if I add that the prior cognitohazard was created by Teals, working your idea in will be a cinch. Edit: At the moment, have decided against this action. Can't pin down why specifically though. Not feeling it enough? Apathy it is.</li>
<li>I was wondering if that wasn't clear enough. Basically, it has no self preservation kind of thing going for it. Multiple stimuli, including audible and physical, were &quot;tested&quot; upon the object, it didn't react to those stimuli. Florence was also retroactively justifying the reaction of Teals a bit. Glad the main point of comedy came off well, but do you recommend thta I change this to be clearer?</li>
<li>Will change.</li>
</ul>
</div>
